**DEFINITION 6.8.** On the category of domains and strict approximable maps a functor $T$ is *continuous on maps* if for any systems $D$ and $E$ the induced mapping

$$
\lambda f.\, T(f) : (D \to_\bot E) \to (T(D) \to_\bot T(E))
$$

is approximable.

The [continuous on maps](1980_continuousOnMaps.md) peer records the library names.

<!-- formalization -->

**Theorem (`definition_68`).** A functor is continuous on maps exactly when, for all domains $D$ and $E$, the induced map on strict approximable functions is approximable with action $T(f)$.

*Proof.* This is the definition packaged as a biconditional. ∎
